<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #e0f2f7; border: 2px solid #a7d9eb; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Amazing Community – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Our Amazing Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> This School Has Been Here a Long Time!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; border-right: none;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b></p>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reading: reading_1: Infer meaning from context clues in short texts.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reading: reading_2: Interpret simple charts and graphs related to texts.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b></p>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reading: Can infer meaning from context clues about community events by identifying time expressions (e.g., 'Tomorrow we will have a community fair.' 'The fair was held last month.').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reading: Can interpret data in charts about community engagement (e.g., 'Ten students like to go to the movies.').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Phonemic Awareness: Can demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words (e.g., recognizing /ʊ/ in 'cook' and /uː/ in 'school').</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows a picture of an old school building or a local community landmark and asks students to guess its age. "Look at this picture. This is a very old school in our community. How old do you think it is? Is it new or old?" The teacher writes key vocabulary from Lesson 1 on the board (e.g., 'community,' 'school,' 'festival,' 'market,' 'teacher'). Students read the words aloud.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to the words on the board. "Today we are going to read about our community. We will look for important words and time expressions." The teacher models how to quickly scan a short sentence for a specific word. For phonemic awareness, the teacher writes 'cook' and 'school' on the board, orally states them, and models identifying the short /ʊ/ and long /uː/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we going to listen or read today?" (Expected answer: "Read!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'school' (/uː/), is the vowel sound short or long?" (Expected answer: "Long!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short blog post titled "Our Amazing Community School: A Look Back!" (narrative excerpt). The text describes the history of a local school, community events held there, and includes references to past and present simple activities with time expressions (e.g., 'every year,' 'last month,' 'now,' 'before'). It also contains a simple bar graph showing "Community Participation at School Events" for different activities (e.g., 'festival,' 'market day,' 'library visit').</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension for specific information and gist.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> Distributes the reading text and tells students to read silently first to get the main idea.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea of this text? Is it about food, or about the history of the school and community?" (Students circle the correct option or raise hands).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students read 3-4 statements related to the text (e.g., "The school is very new." "People gather at the park every year before the festival.") and mark them 'T' or 'F'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Chart Interpretation:</b> Students look at the bar graph and answer, "Which event has the most community participation?" and "Which event has the least?" This checks reading_2.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Inferring meaning from context clues, interpreting charts, identifying short and long vowel sounds, using present simple, past simple, and present perfect with time expressions (e.g., 'yesterday,' 'every day,' 'last month,' 'for five years'). Vocabulary: 'community,' 'event,' 'park,' 'school,' 'festival,' 'teacher,' 'gather,' 'share,' 'cook.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Inferring Meaning & Time Expressions):</b> The teacher provides sentences from the reading text with missing time expressions or key vocabulary words. Students read the sentence and fill in the blank, choosing from a word bank (e.g., "The community has a festival _____ year." Options: 'last,' 'every,' 'now'). This activity directly supports reading_1.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Chart Analysis & Reading Aloud):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a worksheet with 3-4 questions about the bar graph from Stage 2 (e.g., "How many people enjoyed the market day last year?"). They read the questions and interpret the graph to write short answers. The teacher circulates, checking answers and listening to students read the sentences and their answers aloud, providing corrective feedback on pronunciation, especially stress in compound nouns like 'neighborhood' and vowel sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Awareness Practice:</b> The teacher writes a list of single-syllable words from the text on the board (e.g., 'cook,' 'school,' 'meet,' 'greet,' 'park,' 'last,' 'past'). Students read each word silently and then raise a green card for a short vowel sound and a red card for a long vowel sound when the teacher calls out the word. The teacher provides immediate feedback and models pronunciation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Q&A.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a new short text (e.g., a "Community Bulletin Board" notice) about a different local community event or place (e.g., "The Historic Community Market," "Our Neighborhood Library"). Each text is 2-3 paragraphs long and includes a simple pie chart or bar graph about community involvement (e.g., "Favorite Activities at the Library," "Types of Vendors at the Market"). The texts include grammar points like present simple, past simple, and present perfect, and the target vocabulary (e.g., 'vendor,' 'artist,' 'musician,' 'share,' 'discuss,' 'visit').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups read their text and interpret the accompanying chart together.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, each group prepares 3-4 comprehension questions for another group, ensuring some questions require inferring meaning from context clues (reading_1) and others require interpreting the chart (reading_2). (e.g., "What delicious food do people cook at the market every Saturday?" "How long has the artist worked there?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups exchange their texts and questions. They read the new text and answer the questions in writing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, groups discuss their answers, verifying them against the text. The teacher monitors, providing support and noting fluency in reading and accuracy in comprehension.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Observation checklist and written response evaluation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students read a short, new paragraph (about "Our Community Park") which contains specific time expressions (e.g., 'yesterday,' 'every day,' 'next week') and 2-3 target vocabulary words (e.g., 'gardener,' 'volunteer,' 'enjoy'). They then complete 3 multiple-choice or short-answer questions. One question will require inferring meaning from a context clue or time expression, and another will ask them to identify a short or long vowel sound in a word from the text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to correctly infer meaning from context clues in the text (reading_1).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to interpret simple data if a chart was provided (reading_2).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of short/long vowel sounds in target words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy of written answers.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I learned about reading today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can (circle one): understand easy texts / understand some texts / need more help with texts."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading today's stories did I find most interesting?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging for me when reading the texts or charts, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The reading activities allowed most students to practice inferring meaning from context clues and interpreting simple charts. The explicit focus on time expressions was helpful.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students still struggled with identifying specific details in the text, indicating a need for more scanning practice. The phonemic awareness activity was engaging but may require more repetition with different words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Adjustments for next lesson: Incorporate a pre-reading activity that specifically trains scanning for keywords before reading for deeper comprehension. Provide more visual aids for chart interpretation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the information read in this lesson about community events and roles to discuss their own community and participate in a guided interview about community responsibilities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>